    "summary": "Strong classics amid chaotic energy; Dire Wolf & Scarlet sequence shine",
    "blurb": "The Deer Creek show delivers solid versions of Here Comes Sunshine, Dire Wolf and a moody Scarlet Begonias → Fire → Victim stretch. Jerry’s guitar is bright on Let It Grow while the rhythm section drives New Speedway Boogie. Recording quality varies, but the performance holds up despite gate‑crasher interruptions.",
    "review": "On July 2, 1995 at Deer Creek Music Center the Dead pushed through a night of turbulence to give fans several memorable moments. The first set opened with an uplifting Here Comes Sunshine and kept the momentum rolling through Dire Wolf, Desolation Row and a lively Let It Grow that had Jerry “hot as ever,” even if his voice showed signs of fatigue later on. The second‑set centerpiece – Scarlet Begonias into Fire On The Mountain and then Victim Or The Crime – created an eerie, atmospheric journey that many reviewers called the highlight of the night, despite a split‑faded recording of the segue in some tapes. New Speedway Boogie sparked a tight drum groove from Mickey and Bill, leading into a crisp Space segment and a heartfelt Attics Of My Life before closing with Sugar Magnolia and The Mighty Quinn. While gate‑crashers and stray house lights disrupted the vibe and several recordings suffer from cuts or muddy mixes, the band’s resilience shines through in the strong vocal delivery, thumping Phil bass, and solid drumming that kept the show moving forward.\n\nFor listeners seeking the clearest window into this chaotic night, the audience recording gd1995-07-02.139737.cmc5-mk4.ebel-varnum.flac1644 stands out for its excellent front‑of‑house balance and minimal drop‑outs, capturing the energy of the performance without drowning it in technical flaws. Even with the occasional hiccup, this show remains a solid example of mid‑90s Dead, offering plenty of classic material for any fan’s collection.",
